#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060646} :activity/id #uuid "23cd302e-47d0-5453-b9bd-9813f996a502" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060930 :tutorial/content "If we want to multiply one square matrix by another (which has the same dimensions), we multiply each row in the first matrix by each column in the second. \n\nFor example, suppose we want to multiply two matrices \n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        1 & 6  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle B = \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & 0  \\\\\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack.\n$$\n\nThe matrices $A$ and $B$ are both $2 \\times 2$ matrices, and their product will be another $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        1 & 6  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack}_{A}\n\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & 0  \\\\\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack}_{B}\n\n\t&= \n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\ast & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast \n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\t}_{C}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the entries of $C$ (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $c_{11},$ the entry at the intersection of the 1st row and 1st column, we calculate the dot product of the 1st row of $A$ and the 1st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{blue}{1} & \\color{blue}{6}  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{red}{-3} & 0  \\\\\n        \\color{red}{2} & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-3}}) + {\\color{blue}{6}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{12},$ the entry at the intersection of the 1st row and 2nd column, we calculate the dot product of the 1st row of $A$ and the 2nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{blue}{1} & \\color{blue}{6}  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & \\color{red}{0}  \\\\\n        2 & \\color{red}{-5}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{0}} + {\\color{blue}{6}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}})   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{21},$ the entry at the intersection of the 2nd row and 1st column, we calculate the dot product of the 2nd row of $A$ and the 1st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {1} & {6}  \\\\\n        \\color{blue}{4} & \\color{blue}{3}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{red}{-3} & 0  \\\\\n        \\color{red}{2} & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        {\\color{blue}{4}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-3}}) + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{22},$ the entry at the intersection of the 2nd row and 2nd column, we calculate the dot product of the 2nd row of $A$ and the 2nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {1} & {6}  \\\\\n        \\color{blue}{4} & \\color{blue}{3}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & \\color{red}{0}  \\\\\n        2 & \\color{red}{-5}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{0}} + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}})\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & -15\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {1} & {6}  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & 0  \\\\\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & -15\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f1be57fa-6725-5805-b38d-493945b33af9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2989 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1946a4ad-a7ef-5e40-b53e-01ba313b5d21" :step/math-academy-id 16176 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060647}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059555 :question/id #uuid "04e1822f-2da1-5b28-8967-7a0026c39319" :question/math-academy-id "e-4401" :question/problem "Find $AB$ and $BA,$ if \n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\nand\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot (-1) & 1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2 \\\\ -5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-1) & -5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-5) & 0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1 \\\\ -1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-5) & -1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that \n\n$$\nAB \\neq BA.\n$$\n\nMatrix multiplication is not **commutative**, meaning that when we switch the order of multiplication, we often get a different result (like we did in this example)."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dbfd5f3-553b-5276-8716-bbceddefc4d3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696068} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696119}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying 2x2 Square Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "f338c833-45a2-5451-b0e5-90d0a75602ee" :step/math-academy-id 16178 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060727}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059556 :question/id #uuid "3b0c400c-89bd-504d-87c3-e0f24957d42b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4402" :question/problem "Find $AB$, if \n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\nand\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0+0-9 & -6+0-3 & 4+0+6 \\\\ 0-1+6 & 9+2+2 & -6-4-4 \\\\ 0+0+3 & -6+0+1 & 4+0-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -9 & 10 \\\\ 5 & 13 & -14 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c7a77cf-163a-542a-b4a3-4274344b9cf8" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695771} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186592244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186631955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186695850}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying 3x3 Square Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "2835449e-b103-5607-811a-92a9c8f47ae2" :step/math-academy-id 16179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060727 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059800 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059599 :question/id #uuid "4aaa04dd-1380-50c9-8118-52f6e414f830" :question/math-academy-id "e-5255" :question/problem "Let $Z=XY.$ What is the value of $z_{32}$, if\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$X=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$Y=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the $3$rd row in $X$ by the $2$nd column in $Y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{32} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-1+8-6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a827b0d0-faa2-521f-b166-e13873b1da00" :knowledge-point/key-prerequi